 @{"Update or remove" link "MAINTENANCE"} @{"Project information" link "ABOUT"}

 Transfers are unencrypted. Use a trusted local network.
 Computer names and installation IDs are not authenticated identities.

 Open this manual with the Help button in BitSkim or BitSkimPrefs, or
 double-click BitSkim.guide. MultiView is required to display it.
 @{"Updating a running client" link UpdateRestart}
 @{"Accept all files in a batch" link FileBatches}
 @{"Network capabilities" link NetworkCapabilities}
@endnode

@node QUICKSTART "Quick start"

 1. Start your Amiga TCP/IP stack and check that networking works.
 2. Open BitSkim on the Amiga and on another computer on your LAN.
 3. Select the other computer in NEARBY COMPUTERS.
 4. Drop one Workbench file into the main BitSkim window, or choose
    Send file and enter its full Amiga path.
 5. On the receiving computer, accept the offer and choose where to save.
 6. Wait for successful delivery confirmation. A full progress bar alone
    is not the sender's final delivery confirmation.

 If discovery does not find the other computer, enter its IPv4 address
 in IP or IP:port and press Return, then send. The other computer must
 be reachable and running BitSkim.

 The Preferences program is installed in SYS:Prefs by default. The default
 theme is Catppuccin Mocha; neither app opens a private full-screen view.

 @{"Installation" link "INSTALL"}  @{"Sending" link "SENDING"}  @{"Receiving" link "RECEIVING"}
@endnode

@node REQUIREMENTS "Requirements"

 - AmigaOS 3.x with Workbench, at least 640 x 256 pixels.
 - An AmiTCP-compatible TCP/IP stack exposing bsdsocket.library v4+.
   Roadshow is the initial real-stack target.
 - A working network interface and route to the other computer.
 - Writable storage for received files and enough free space.
 - MultiView for this AmigaGuide manual.
 - The system Installer v42+ for the optional installation script.

 The executable targets the 68000 instruction set. Minimum RAM and real
 68000 performance have not been certified. Startup checks do not
 establish compatibility with every Amiga/network stack.

 Other clients include Apple silicon macOS and Windows 10 x64. RISC OS
 ARM2 and StrongARM builds are experimental and not runtime certified.
 Use short Latin-1 filenames for transfers involving classic machines.

 Networking depends on the configured TCP/IP stack. BitSkim
 does not install or reconfigure Roadshow, an Ethernet driver or a bridge.
 Network setup tools are separate from the BitSkim package.

 @{"Network discovery" link "DISCOVERY"}  @{"Troubleshooting" link "TROUBLE"}
@endnode

@node INSTALL "Install on Workbench"

 Unpack the entire Amiga ZIP. Keep the outer BitSkim.info beside the
 BitSkim drawer; it is the drawer's icon. Inside are the client,
 BitSkimPrefs, the Installer, its launcher, this guide and their icons.

 Double-click Install-BitSkim. The project icon runs StartInstaller,
 which prefers SYS:System/Installer and falls back to C:Installer.
 Close BitSkimPrefs before installing or updating.

 Novice mode uses the standard paths automatically, adds BitSkim to
 WBStartup and launches it after installing. Choose Average/Expert in
 the Installer welcome screen for custom installation. Intermediate
 chooses the two executable drawers; Expert also chooses shared data.
 Both ask about boot startup. Fresh installs also ask about launching;
 updates automatically stop the clients and restart the installed app.
 See @{"Updating a running client" link UpdateRestart}. Default locations:

   Main app drawer: SYS:Utilities (executable and icon directly here)
   Prefs drawer:    SYS:Prefs     (Preferences executable and icon)
   Shared parent:   SYS:          (creates iconless SYS:BitSkim/)

 Shared Plugins, Tools, Themes and Images reserve future resources.
 The current client has no plugin/skin loader; artwork remains embedded.
 No shared drawer .info is installed. Show All Files can still reveal it.

 Fixed locations:
   HELP:BitSkim.guide     guide and its project icon
   ENV:BitSkim/BitSkim.prefs      live settings
   ENVARC:BitSkim/BitSkim.prefs   permanent settings

 Settings project icons open the selected BitSkimPrefs executable.
 Both programs find Help through the installation records. Keep HELP:
 available; the installer creates no assign and edits no startup scripts.
 Installer tools stay in the unpacked package, alongside InstallAssets/.

 Recorded installations offer only Update/Uninstall. Updates retain the
 three recorded app/data locations, migrate the guide to HELP:,
 and migrate saved settings without replacing an existing new archive.
 Partial records stop the operation. Custom unregistered copies are not
 disk-scanned; standard older layouts use their documented locations automatically.

 A leftover SYS:Utilities/BitSkim drawer blocks the direct executable path.
 Installation preserves it as BitSkim.previous, including unknown files
 and its icon. If that backup already exists, installation stops without
 overwriting it. Run the installer from the separate Bin distribution.

 Portable alternative: keep the complete unpacked drawer together.

 Shell installation alternative, from the unpacked package drawer:

   Installer Install-BitSkim APPNAME BitSkim

 The Installer preserves settings and received files. It does not install
 a TCP/IP stack, add an assign or edit startup scripts. Novice mode adds
 an owned WBStartup launcher and starts the app automatically.
 Novice starts the client automatically. Intermediate/Expert ask whether to launch after installing. SYS:WBStartup/BitSkimStart reads the recorded application path;
 it does not contain a second copy of the main app. Its DONOTWAIT icon
 avoids waiting for the app to quit during boot. Uninstall removes this
 entry only when ENVARC:BitSkim/startup-owned proves installer ownership.
 Networking must already be available when the app starts.

 @{"Update/removal" link "MAINTENANCE"}  @{"Files in the package" link "FILES"}
@endnode

@node DISCOVERY "Find a computer"

 Nearby computers normally appear automatically on the same IPv4 subnet.
 Select a row, then send or drop a file. Use the up/down controls to
 browse more than five nearby entries. Refresh requests discovery now.

 Discovery uses UDP 46440. File transfers use TCP, normally port 46440.
 Firewalls, guest Wi-Fi isolation, VPNs and NAT can prevent
 broadcast discovery or incoming connections.

 To connect manually, enter an address in IP or IP:port and press Return:

   172.16.1.20
   172.16.1.20:46441

 Use the port shown by the receiving client. Do not enter a web URL.
 Manual IP bypasses discovery, not routing or firewall restrictions.

 The Amiga listener can be selected from Shell:

   Bin:BitSkim/BitSkim PORT=46441

 A default-port conflict may cause the Amiga client to try 46441. Read
 the actual port in its footer. Discovery remains on UDP 46440.

 Idle entries normally expire after 90 seconds. Certain connection
 failures remove a peer sooner; fresh discovery can add it again.

 While iconified, outgoing searches stop and the local peer list clears.
 Restore the window to resume discovery. This is an optimization.

 @{"Background operation" link "BACKGROUND"}  @{"No peers found" link "TROUBLE"}
@endnode

@node SENDING "Send a file"

 Select a nearby peer. Choose Send file to open a nonblocking path window,
 or find a file in Workbench and drop it into BitSkim's main window.
 The path window shows its recipient. Enter a complete Amiga filename:

   Work:Files/example.txt

 Clicking Send in that path window uses its displayed recipient, even if
 the main selection later changes. A drop into the main window uses the
 main window's current selection. Only one regular file is supported.
 Directories and multiple-file drops are rejected.

 For a discovered recipient, BitSkim retains its stable installation ID
 and checks the remote HELLO identity before offering the file. A missing
 or ambiguous ID is an error; it does not silently choose another peer.
 Explicit manual-IP sending remains an address-based operation.

 The receiver must accept and choose a destination according to its own
 preferences. Sending does not bypass consent or overwrite protection.
 Avoid changing the source file until the transfer finishes.

 Progress counts payload written to the local connection. Delivery is
 confirmed only by the receiver's final checksum/save acknowledgement.

 @{"Progress/cancel" link "PROGRESS"}  @{"Filename limits" link "SAFETY"}
@endnode

@node RECEIVING "Receive a file"

 By default an incoming offer opens a themed, nonblocking window showing
 the sender label, filename, size and proposed full destination path.
 Review it and choose Accept or Decline. Closing the offer declines it.

 Edit the full save path if you want a different drawer or filename:

   Work:Downloads/example.txt

 No destination is created before acceptance. BitSkim receives into a
 temporary file, checks CRC-32 and finalizes without silently replacing
 an existing destination. A collision may select a numbered alternative;
 on old filesystems, keep the final leafname within their length limit.

 Networking, discovery and ARexx continue while the offer/path window is
 open. Remote cancellation or timeout invalidates and closes a stale
 offer, so a late click cannot accept an old transfer.

 In General preferences (Allow incoming is in Network):

 - Allow incoming transfers controls whether offers can be received.
 - Automatically accept offers avoids the manual decision only when
   Always ask where to save is off.
 - Always ask still opens the editable save-path window with auto-accept.
 - Choose a persistent folder for unattended reception. RAM: is volatile.

 Auto-accept with Always ask disabled can receive while iconified.
 Files are never opened automatically after reception.

 @{"Receiving preferences" link "PREFERENCES"}  @{"Safety" link "SAFETY"}
@endnode

@node PROGRESS "Progress, cancellation and delivery"

 During sending/receiving the client shows transferred and total size,
 percentage, remaining data and recent payload speed. Sizes use binary
 units (KiB/MiB/GiB). Early speed samples or a stall can show zero.

 The engine streams small chunks rather than buffering the whole file.
 Only one transfer is active at a time. Window redraws are throttled on
 the Amiga to reduce work on classic CPUs.

 Cancel stops the active transfer and removes its incomplete receive
 file. A best-effort CANCEL frame informs the other peer; a broken or
 blocked connection can instead appear as a connection failure there.
 Closing a send-path selection window just cancels that selection.

 Recipient decisions time out after 120 seconds. Other connection
 inactivity times out after about 30 seconds. Detection is not instant.

 If completion is unconfirmed, the receiver may already have saved the
 file before its acknowledgement was lost. Check there before retrying.
 CRC-32 detects accidental corruption; it is not cryptographic protection.

 @{"Background" link "BACKGROUND"}  @{"Troubleshooting" link "TROUBLE"}
@endnode

@node PREFERENCES "Preferences"

 Open Preferences in the client, or start BitSkimPrefs from SYS:Prefs.
 The editor works without a TCP/IP stack and does not open a listener.
 Its sections are General, Appearance, Network and About.

 GENERAL
   Client name: how this installation is labelled on the LAN. The initial
   name comes from the hostname. Keep it short; the protocol permits
   63 UTF-8 bytes, which can be fewer than 63 non-ASCII characters.

   Start iconified: begins with a Workbench AppIcon on the next launch.
   Close window to iconify: keeps the client running when Close is used.
   Both iconify options default off and are enabled only by your choice.
   Use Quit to exit explicitly in close-to-iconify mode.

   Automatically accept offers, Always ask where to save and Folder
   control file acceptance and destination. Auto-accept respects Always ask.

 APPEARANCE
   Select a theme and inspect its preview. Use or Save applies the choice.

 NETWORK
   Visible on network controls discovery advertising. Visibility is not
   access control: manual-IP reception can remain enabled while hidden.
   Allow incoming transfers gates all incoming file offers.

 ABOUT
   Version, project repository and planned website.

 Use writes only live ENV: settings and applies them for this session.
 Save writes ENV: and ENVARC: for this session and future boots. Revert restores the opening values. Cancel or
 closing the editor discards changes. Saving signals the primary BITSKIM
 host to reload when idle; a transfer is not reconfigured halfway through.
 Secondary hosts can use ARexx RELOAD explicitly. The last saved editor
 wins if multiple copies are open.

 Installed live settings use ENV:BitSkim/BitSkim.prefs; saved settings use
 ENVARC:BitSkim/BitSkim.prefs. Older flat/shared settings are read only
 as fallback when canonical settings are absent.
 The Help button opens this guide without discarding the current draft.

 @{"Themes" link "THEMES"}  @{"Background" link "BACKGROUND"}
@endnode

@node THEMES "Themes and Workbench windows"

 Eight choices are available:

   Catppuccin Latte       Catppuccin Frappe
   Catppuccin Macchiato   Catppuccin Mocha (default)
   BitSkim Rosewater      BitSkim Mint
   Arctic                Classic

 In Appearance, click the theme selector to cycle forward; Shift-click
 cycles backward. The preview is illustrative and performs no transfer.
 Use or Save applies the selected theme.

 Both applications use ordinary Workbench windows. Content, buttons,
 selectors and peer rows use application pens. Intuition title bars and
 system dialogs follow Workbench styling. No private screen is opened
 and no other application's colours are changed.

 Low-colour screens approximate the palette using available pens.
 Classic uses Workbench colours. Modeless transfer windows own their
 screen locks/pens, independently of the main window's lifetime.

 @{"Preferences" link "PREFERENCES"}  @{"Project information" link "ABOUT"}
@endnode

@node BACKGROUND "Iconify and background operation"

 Iconify hides the main window behind a Workbench AppIcon. Double-click
 that icon to restore it. Optional Start iconified and Close window to
 iconify settings are in General preferences.

 While hidden, BitSkim keeps incoming transfers, discovery replies,
 ARexx commands and event history active. It pauses outgoing peer searches,
 clears the cached peer list and avoids unnecessary UI work. PEERS=0 in
 STATUS is therefore normal until discovery resumes on restore.

 SHOW restores and refreshes discovery. REFRESH alone does not override
 the iconified search policy. SENDID requires the peer to be in the current
 catalog; manual-address SEND does not require a catalog entry.

 An offer needing attention can show the main window and its modeless
 offer dialog. An unattended accepted transfer can remain iconified.
 Existing modeless windows have independent resources; hiding the main
 window does not invalidate them.

 Use Quit, ARexx QUIT or a Shell Ctrl-C break to exit. Incoming reception
 stops when the process exits. Iconify is not an operating-system login
 item and does not itself add anything to WBStartup.

 @{"ARexx" link "AREXX"}  @{"NovaDock" link "NOVADOCK"}
@endnode

@node AREXX "Local ARexx commands"

 With rexxsyslib.library available the client registers BITSKIM, or
 BITSKIM.1 through BITSKIM.99 if names are occupied. The port remains
 available while iconified. Run RexxMast to execute scripts using RX.

 Example script:

   OPTIONS RESULTS
   ADDRESS BITSKIM 'STATUS'
   IF RC = 0 THEN SAY RESULT

 Supplied examples live in ARexx/ beside the application:

   RX Bin:BitSkim/ARexx/Status.rexx
   RX Bin:BitSkim/ARexx/DockStatus.rexx

 Queries:
   VERSION       application version (0.1)
   CAPABILITIES  independent ARexx extension version and feature flags
   STATUS        decimal KEY=VALUE progress/policy fields, including BPS
   SESSION       session-generation token
   DETAILS       current/most recently started transfer details
   LAST          retained most recent terminal transfer result
   PEERS         stable advertised peer IDs
   PEERINFO id   escaped details for one stable ID
   EVENTS seq    legacy retained event list
   EVENTDETAILS seq  session-aware events with IDs/results
   ERROR         diagnostic for the most recent failed command

 Actions:
   SHOW / HIDE                 restore / iconify
   PREFS / RELOAD              open preferences / reload when idle
   REFRESH                     request discovery when searches are active
   VISIBLE ON / OFF            session visibility policy
   RECEIVING ON / OFF          session incoming-transfer policy
   SEND ip[:port] "Amiga:path"  address-based send
   SENDID id "Amiga:path"      stable-ID send
   CANCEL / QUIT               cancel transfer / exit

 Commands are case insensitive. Quoted arguments support spaces; double
 the matching quote to include it. Backslashes are literal, not escapes.
 Native command paths use Amiga Latin-1 bytes, not UTF-8.

 RC 0 means success/accepted, 10 bad command/arguments, 20 failure/busy/
 unavailable/shutdown. Sending is asynchronous: accepted is not delivered.
 On failure no result Argstring is returned; query ERROR for diagnostics.

 Legacy PEER index remains available but is unsafe for a retained recipient
 selection because discovery can reorder entries. Use stable IDs instead.
 Management is local only, never exposed over the file-transfer protocol.
 There is no ARexx command to bypass normal incoming acceptance policy.

 @{"Details/events" link "DETAILS"}  @{"NovaDock" link "NOVADOCK"}
@endnode

@node DETAILS "ARexx details, outcomes and events"

 STATUS contains numeric decimal key=value tokens separated by whitespace.
 It never contains free-text names or errors. Unknown fields should be
 tolerated. BPS is recent payload bytes/second; zero is inactive/unknown.

 DETAILS and LAST have AVAILABLE=0 when no record exists. Otherwise they
 include SESSION, qualified ID, ACTIVE, INCOMING, BYTES, TOTAL, RESULT,
 FILENAME, PEER, ADDRESS and ERROR. LAST stays readable while idle and
 during the next transfer; the next terminal result replaces it.

 Results:
   0 pending   1 complete   2 failed   3 cancelled   4 declined

 Text values use byte-wise percent encoding. Decode once. Letters,
 digits, hyphen, period, underscore and tilde are literal; other bytes
 are %HH. Empty values follow '=' directly. '+' is not a space.
 Names/filenames are UTF-8; diagnostics are opaque bytes and can contain
 invalid UTF-8 from a remote peer. Never execute diagnostic text.

 EVENTS returns LATEST/LOST and sequence:type pairs. EVENTDETAILS also
 includes SESSION and sequence:type:qualified-id:result entries.
 Types: 1 peers changed, 2 offer, 3 status, 4 complete, 5 error.
 A terminal result can first appear on a status event, including decline.

 The newest 32 events are kept without being consumed by reads. Fast
 progress can overflow history. LOST=1 or a changed SESSION requires
 resynchronization using STATUS and LAST. Counters start at 1; generation
 changes before 32-bit sequence/counter reuse. Restart clears history and
 snapshots. Session/transfer IDs are local observation tokens, not security.

 Maximum complete command: 1199 bytes; successful response: 4095 bytes.
 For the complete machine contract, see ARexx/NOVADOCK.md in the package
 or docs/NOVADOCK.md in source. ERROR is host-global and another client
 can replace that diagnostic; transfer IDs/results are authoritative.

 @{"Commands" link "AREXX"}  @{"NovaDock" link "NOVADOCK"}
@endnode

@node NOVADOCK "NovaDock and other applications"

 NovaDock can discover BITSKIM or a numbered host and query capabilities.
 Existing integrations using numeric STATUS, SHOW, REFRESH, RECEIVING,
 PREFS and CANCEL remain supported. ARexx protocol 2 adds optional details,
 retained results, session-aware events and stable-ID recipient APIs.

 A plugin should hold at most one asynchronous request in flight and keep
 each RexxMsg/argument alive until the reply arrives, even after its own
 timeout. A timeout is not proof that a transfer failed.

 Suggested polling:
   1. CAPABILITIES and STATUS.
   2. EVENTDETAILS with a private cursor; compare SESSION every time.
   3. On LOST/session change, resynchronize with STATUS and LAST.
   4. Deduplicate outcome badges using LAST's qualified transfer ID.
   5. For a future recipient picker, use PEERS, PEERINFO and SENDID.

 Modeless transfer windows keep the client task servicing ARexx. Native
 startup errors before host registration and the separate preferences
 program are different from the running client's management loop.

 The dock plugin decides how to display icons, badges and notifications.
 A direct drop onto a dock tile depends on that plugin's implementation;
 installing BitSkim alone does not implement it inside another product.
 No NovaDock plugin binary or network/bridge tool is installed here.

 @{"Details/events" link "DETAILS"}  @{"Troubleshooting" link "TROUBLE"}
@endnode

@node TROUBLE "Troubleshooting"

 Cannot open bsdsocket.library:
   Start a compatible TCP/IP stack first. In test execution, ensure the chosen
   networking method really provides the guest stack/interface you intend.

 No peers appear:
   Restore the client if iconified, then Refresh. Check visibility and
   receiving policy on the recipient. Try its explicit IP/port. Verify
   subnet, route, firewall and Wi-Fi isolation on both computers.

 Port busy or wrong endpoint:
   Close duplicate processes or choose PORT= from Shell. Read the actual
   TCP port in the footer, especially when testing on a shared host.

 Path/filename rejected:
   Use a complete native path and an existing writable drawer. Incoming
   Amiga leafnames must be Latin-1 and at most 30 characters. Unsupported
   Unicode names need renaming on the sender. A numbered collision suffix
   also needs to fit the old filesystem's limit.

 Cannot create/finalize a file:
   Check free space, write protection, volume availability and the final
   filename. BitSkim does not silently overwrite an existing destination.
   On modern filesystems lacking safe finalization primitives it fails.

 Waiting, timeout or unconfirmed delivery:
   Check the other host and link. The receiver may already have saved a
   completed file whose final acknowledgement was lost. Verify before retry.

 Preferences did not change the running copy:
   Wait until the active transfer finishes. Saving signals the primary
   BITSKIM host; send RELOAD to a numbered secondary host explicitly.

 Help does not open:
   Keep the guide in the selected help drawer and reinstall if locations
   have moved. Portable copies can keep it beside the apps. Check MultiView is
   available as SYS:Utilities/MultiView or C:MultiView. You can open the
   guide manually. Its .info is a project icon, not the client executable.

 Native Amiga dialogs, networking and installers need guest/
 hardware checks; host tests and successful builds do not certify them.

 @{"Requirements" link "REQUIREMENTS"}  @{"Safety" link "SAFETY"}
@endnode

@node SAFETY "Limits and safety"

 BitSkim 0.1 transfers are unencrypted. Names, IPs and installation IDs
 are not authenticated. Stable-ID checks prevent accidental recipient
 changes; they do not provide cryptographic pairing. Use a trusted LAN
 and do not forward the listening port to the public Internet.

 CRC-32 detects accidental corruption, not malicious modification.
 Received files are never executed or opened automatically.

 One regular file per transfer, one active transfer per client, maximum
 2,147,483,647 bytes. No directories, resume or encryption on Amiga. The queue uses temporary storage; see FileBatches.
 Files are byte-preserving; platform metadata, Amiga protection bits and
 automatic companion .info files are not bundled. Send an .info separately
 if desired. Never assume a downloaded file is safe just because it arrived.

 Incoming Amiga filenames are limited to 30 Latin-1 characters. Other
 receivers impose their own filesystem limits. RISC OS proposed leafnames
 are shortened for old FileCore; that client is experimental.

 Very large files, full/removable volumes, slow real hardware and routed
 networks require further validation. The shipped validation notes/source
 build records distinguish tested behavior from intended compatibility.

 @{"Progress" link "PROGRESS"}  @{"Project information" link "ABOUT"}
@endnode

@node FILES "Files and settings"

 In the unpacked distribution package:
   BitSkim                 main Workbench client
   BitSkim.info            main application icon
   BitSkimPrefs             separate preferences editor
   BitSkimPrefs.info        preferences icon
   BitSkim.guide            this AmigaGuide manual
   BitSkim.guide.info       manual project icon (MultiView)
   Install-BitSkim          Installer script
   Install-BitSkim.info     installer project icon (StartInstaller)
   StartInstaller           system Installer launcher
   StartBitSkim              small WBStartup launcher template
   ARexx/                   example scripts and integration contract
   BUILD-PROVENANCE.json    source/toolchain/payload build record
   README.MD, INSTALLATION.md, LICENSE and third-party notices

 Installation-location files live in ENV:BitSkim/ and ENVARC:BitSkim/:
   app.path, prefs.path, shared.path, guide.path
 These contain literal selected paths, never Shell commands.

 The Installer and its launcher are not copied into installed locations.
 Keep the distribution package to update/remove later.

 The outer BitSkim.info belongs beside the drawer, not inside it.
 The inner BitSkim.info belongs beside the main executable.
 The drawer and installer use the supplied software-package/install art.

 Settings:
   ENV:BitSkim/BitSkim.prefs       installed live settings
   ENV:BitSkim.prefs        legacy/portable live settings
   ENVARC:BitSkim/BitSkim.prefs   permanent settings
   ENVARC:BitSkim.prefs     legacy/portable persistent settings

 Keep the installation ID unique to each computer. Copying another
 machine's saved settings can duplicate its advertised ID.
 Received files go to the configured/chosen path, not necessarily here.
 RAM: contents disappear on restart.

 @{"Install" link "INSTALL"}  @{"Update/remove" link "MAINTENANCE"}
@endnode

@node MAINTENANCE "Update and remove"

 Close BitSkim (including iconified copies) and BitSkimPrefs first.
 Run Install-BitSkim from the newly unpacked full archive. Check the
 installer-displayed directories, then Update BitSkim. Registered copies
 retain the three selectable paths without destination questions.
 Help and settings use their fixed system locations.

 To remove, select Uninstall BitSkim for the displayed installation. Only known bundled
 files, including the manual and its icon, are removed. Settings, received
 files, unknown files and the drawer itself remain. Remove empty leftover
 drawers manually if desired.

 Manual update is also possible by copying the new complete package.
 Portable copies can stay together. Installed copies use the central
 location metadata. Keep the installer/launcher package for maintenance.
 Avoid replacing files while a transfer is active.

 Version stays 0.1 until the owner requests a version change. New packages
 carry source and payload fingerprints to avoid mixing old binaries with
 new source. Build records are not publisher signatures or authentication.

 @{"Package files" link "FILES"}  @{"Project information" link "ABOUT"}
@endnode

@node ABOUT "About BitSkim"

 BitSkim 0.1 - first public release in preparation.
 Native interfaces share a small, bounded, nonblocking C transfer engine.

 Source repository:
   https://github.com/retrocs-org/bitskim

 Planned public website (future launch):
   https://bitskim.retrocs.org

 Source code and project-generated art: see the included MIT license.
 Supplied artwork and third-party components have their recorded notices.
 Catppuccin palette credits and the NovaDock-derived installer launcher's
 license are included with the package. No platform-vendor affiliation
 or Apple AirDrop interoperability is implied.

 The application uses no accounts or cloud relay. Modern macOS/Windows TLS,
 authenticated pairing and broader platform features require separately
 designed extensions; they are not enabled by an option in this version.

 This manual uses plain ASCII and local AmigaGuide links for classic
 Workbench viewers. The URLs above are informational, not shell actions.

 @{"Contents" link "MAIN"}  @{"Quick start" link "QUICKSTART"}
@endnode


@node UpdateRestart "Updating a running client"
 Close BitSkimPrefs before upgrading. Update automatically requests normal
 shutdown of all registered BitSkim clients. Active transfers are cancelled
 and waiting queues are discarded. If shutdown takes over ten seconds,
 update aborts before replacing program files.

 After a successful upgrade, one copy of the installed app starts using its
 saved preferences. Failed or aborted updates do not launch the app.
 StopBitSkim is a package-only helper and is not installed.
@endnode

@node NetworkCapabilities "Network capabilities"
 Every current client announces its supported transfer features and limits
 in broadcast discovery, then confirms them on the live connection.
 Amiga reports plaintext only, a 2 GiB-minus-one per-file limit, UTF-8 wire
 names up to 255 bytes, and mapped Latin-1 save names up to 30 bytes.
 Collections use one browsable list and one destination folder.
 Modern Mac and Windows clients prefer automatic TLS when possible and
 otherwise allow Amiga plaintext. Their Force encryption setting prevents
 transfers with this plaintext-only Amiga implementation. No directories,
 active-file pause, TLS or interrupted-file resume are advertised by Amiga.
 ARexx NETWORKCAPS reports the local numeric capability profile.
@endnode

@node FileBatches "Accept all files in a batch"
 Updated senders offer the complete list for one recipient. Browse with
 Previous/Next, then Accept all and select one folder. Temporary storage
 is required (T: normally uses RAM). Count ceiling: 32760 files.
 BitSkim shows the proposed local filenames and sizes. Names may be adapted
 for the Amiga. Choose the existing folder in Save all in folder, then
 Accept all. Each following listed file uses that folder without asking
 again. Existing files are preserved and collisions get numbered names.

 This permission applies only to this list, sender and order. Cancellation,
 errors, changed offers or a two-minute gap between files require fresh
 consent. Additional files added later are a new offer. Older clients
 still ask per file. No folders are transferred and archives are not
 automatically unpacked. Batch files currently restart from the beginning
 after interruption; modern single-file resume is a separate feature.
@endnode

@node TOOLS "Transfer tools, conflicts and history"
 Hold the right mouse button and choose Tools / Transfers / Diagnostics.
 The modeless window keeps transfers and ARexx running. Page scrolls;
 History toggles diagnostics and recent results; Older cycles the last
 eight results; Retry queues an unsuccessful outgoing file to its saved
 stable peer ID. The path and result remain available until exit.

 Conflicts also appears in incoming offers and collection approvals.
 It cycles Rename (default), Skip and Replace for this session.
 It applies to the remaining files of an approved collection. Rename
 preserves extensions and chooses an unused name. Skip needs an updated
 sender; otherwise rename or decline. Replace checks the complete new
 file before moving the original to a same-folder BK backup and committing.
 If finalization fails and restoration is blocked, retain that backup.
 Explicit Replace may replace an earlier duplicate; Rename retains all.

 Single-file and collection suggestions share conservative Amiga mapping:
 Latin-1-compatible names, 30 bytes and retained extensions where possible.
 This is safe on older filesystems even when the OS supports longer names.
 Modern device pairing is unavailable on the plaintext Amiga client.

 Updated peers negotiate written-file CRC verification. The temporary
 file is read back in bounded slices before final commit and acknowledgement.
 Legacy transfers retain the original streamed CRC/receipt protocol.
 CRC detects accidental damage, not malicious network modification.

 Diagnostics show the stack API, bind/listen ports, peer endpoint, caps,
 limits and last error. They do not start or reconfigure Roadshow or UAE.
@endnode

@node FOLDERS "Folders and disk space"

 Send and receive dialogs remember separate last-used folders. Missing
 folders fall back to the configured default. History is saved locally in
 ENVARC:BitSkim/BSFOLD.DAT.

 Progress includes the current file position, overall payload bytes and
 speed. Cancel discards the remaining files and removes the current partial
 file; completed files stay saved.

 Transfer tools includes Test network. It checks the entered or most recent
 peer over TCP without sending a file. See Diagnostics for the result.

 Drop a Workbench drawer or enter its path to send a folder. Nested and
 empty directories are included. Updated peers show the whole list and
 approve it once into a chosen parent folder. A fresh, uniquely named root
 is created; existing folders are never merged or replaced. Older clients
 cannot receive folders. Files still stream one at a time.

 Tree previews wrap two paths per page. Names are adapted for the receiver;
 Amiga defaults remain conservative at 30 bytes per name. Relative wire
 paths allow 255 UTF-8 bytes and 16 components. Queue capacity includes
 directories. Links, special files and filesystem metadata are not sent.
 Cancel leaves created folders and completed files intact. A retry creates
 another safe root rather than merging into the previous partial folder.

 The incoming dialog shows estimated required and available disk space.
 Checks run before approval and before every file. The estimate includes
 allocation overhead and a reserve; it does not credit replaced or skipped
 files. An unavailable space query is shown explicitly and does not block
 receiving. Space is not reserved, so later writes can still fail.
 Temporary queue and manifest spools also need free disk space.
@endnode

@node RESULTS "Collection results and retry"

 Before approval, BitSkim shows file count, folder count and payload size.
 Transfer tools retains the latest incoming and outgoing result counts.
 Queue / Retry failed only rebuilds failed payloads when the queue is empty.
 Completed, skipped and cancelled files are excluded. A folder retry makes
 a fresh root with only the needed parent directories. A failed folder scan
 needs an explicit requeue. Check the receiver after an unconfirmed result.
 Enable Completion beep in General to announce successful delivery.
@endnode
